<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iuput (High Priest of Amun)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">10th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Iuput was an Egyptian prince, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shoshenq-i">Shoshenq I</a>, founder of the Twenty-second Dynasty. Around 943 BCE his father appointed him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/high-priest-of-amun">High Priest of Amun</a> at Thebes, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> and army commander, bringing the Theban priesthood under royal control. Inscriptions at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> record his role, including in his father's building works.</p></div>
